{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Diagramming a Business Process "}, {"type": "position", "content": 12}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear and use when talking about diagramming business processes. "}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understands systems theories, business processes and basic concepts on IT.  Create a map of processes that allows identifying areas in an information system, using IT tools to generate reports based on the company's needs."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands key information in written texts about the communicative topics related to this level.   Exchanges simple information on IT concepts, systems theory, and diagrams of processes."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n1le01ob12re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Present Continuous: Current Actions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the <span class= \"strong\">present continuous </span>  for things that are happening at the very moment you talk or for temporary situations that are happening around now (but not at this exact moment)."}, {"type": "text", "content": "One way in which you use the <span class= \"strong\">present continuous</span>   is to talk about things you are doing at the very moment of speaking.<br><br>  This is the structure you use for the <span class= \"strong\">present continuous</span> : <br><br>  Subject + <span class= \"strong\">verb to be</span>   (am-is-are) + <span class= \"strong\">verb \u2013ing</span>   + complement. <br><br>  Remember you have to choose the correct form of the verb to be depending on the subject: <span class= \"strong\">am</span>   for the first person singular, is for the third person singular, and <span class= \"strong\">are</span>   for the remaining ones. <br><br>  Example: <br> <span class= \"italic\"> I <span class= \"strong\">am designing</span>   the diagram right now. </span> <br><br>   Another use of the <span class= \"strong\">present continuous</span>   is to talk about extended actions that take place <span class= \"strong\">now</span>   but not at this very moment. Remember that <span class= \"strong\">now</span>   can mean this hour, today, this week, this month, this year, and so on; not only this very second or minute. <br><br>  Example: <br> <span class= \"italic\"> Diagramming <span class= \"strong\">is becoming</span>   a relevant technique. </span> <br> (It is not  happening right now; it is a temporary condition up to the present) <br><br>   Don't forget the spelling of the verbs when adding the suffix -ing: "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<div class=\"center_text\"> <span class= \"strong\">ADDING  -ING </span>   </div>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":5,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class= \\\"strong\\\">When the verb... </span>   \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class= \\\"strong\\\">Example: </span>   \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Ends in <span class= \\\"strong\\\">e </span>  you drop the e and add <span class= \\\"strong\\\">\u2013ing</span> :            \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"write- writing\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Ends in <span class= \\\"strong\\\">ee</span>  you just add \u2013ing:                             \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"see- seeing\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Ends in <span class= \\\"strong\\\">ie</span>  you change it by <span class= \\\"strong\\\">y</span> , then add <span class= \\\"strong\\\">\u2013ing     \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"lie - lying die- dying\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Ends in consonant vowel consonant you double the consonant ( X,Y and Z are an exception to this rule)                 \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"set- setting stop-stopping\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I'm reading this book about business, and I have a good idea to implement this information into our current business process diagram for the company."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Many companies are currently using them to represent their business processes graphically."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I am currently organizing everything to start this part of the process."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Tips To Diagram A Business Process"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The action of diagramming a business process allows organizing every people involved in it. It helps to simplify and represent the tasks and actions assigned to every person or actor in the business, including the interaction and multiple roles."}, {"type": "text", "content": "These are some tips to apply when designing a diagram to represent a business process:"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Simplified Form Of A Business Process Diagram\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"1.\\tIt is useful to understand the business process first and then diagram the steps.<br> 2.\\tIdentify actors and put them in a swim lane. (actors can be either people or a computer) <br>  3.\\tIdentify the actors' roles in each stage of the business process. <br> 4.\\tBy using swim lanes, you can relate business actors' performance and roles. <br> 5.\\tThe process flow can start at the top and continue going down. <br> 6.\\tUse arrows to represent connection between actions. <br> 7.\\tDiamonds represent decision points. <br> 8.\\tSometimes, it is useful to delete non-essential elements, to make the diagram easy to read. <br>\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "implement "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to put a plan into operation"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I have a good idea to implement this information into our current business process diagram for the company. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le01ob12aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "swim lane "}, {"type": "definition", "content": " a row or column in a table; this name comes from the lanes you find in swimming pools"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: As you can see, each actor is standing on a swim lane. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le01ob12aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "both "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "including one and the other; the former and the latter as well"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: With this diagram, we can show both of these processes.   "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le01ob12aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "barrier "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an obstacle; something that does not allow you to get or do something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: From that information, we can determine the current barriers and the possible solutions to overcome them.  "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le01ob12aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "overcome "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to prevail over a bad circumstance"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: From that information, we can determine the current barriers and the possible solutions to overcome them.  "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n1le01ob12aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for information about one company, you can choose what you want."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Describe what the company is about and what it offers."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write what actors that company has, what actions they can do, and what the best way to be successful is."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your diagram with the class, and discuss about what the most appropriate diagram is."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I find information about a company."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I identify what a company produces."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I recognize different actors and actions inside a company."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can understand written information about how to diagram a business process."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can answer questions related to diagramming a business process."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can write very simple ideas for a business diagram."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Do you use diagrams to study? Why?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What computer program can you use to draw a diagram?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "co